AB  - As Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) and the realities of climate change become widely accepted around the world, the next-generation of integrated pest management will become even more important for establishing a sustainable food production system. To meet the current challenge of food security and climate change, biological control has been developed as one sustainable crop protection technology. However, most registered bacteria are ubiquitous soil-borne bacteria that are closely related to food poisoning and spoilage bacteria. Therefore, this review outlined (1) the mechanism of action of bacterial pesticides, (2) potential concerns about secondary contamination sources associated with past food contamination, and, as a prospective solution, focused on (3) principles and methods of bacterial identification, and (4) the possibility of identifying residual bacteria based on mass spectrometry.
